# A notion, not a class

**A notion, not a class** is a north star: an abstraction over claims that several concept cards
make each in its own terms. A strange loop is an interpretive notion fixed by examples and not a defined class of objects, so the vault's own definitions of it are proposals and say so. It is the vault's account of its own standing.

A north star is proposed and not found in the discussion. Its own claim is
graded `proposed`. How stars are chosen and checked is described in the
project's `methodology/` folder.

## What it abstracts

These claims are about the status of other claims. Hofstadter defined the
strange loop by example; only his sense is established usage; the taxonomy
replaces the attempt at one definition; the reflective formal system fits the
one case it was written for; the two rigorous examples work by different
mechanisms. In atoms: `has(strange-loop, formal-definition)` is denied,
`example-of(godel-sentence, strange-loop)` and
`example-of(constructible-universe, strange-loop)` are asserted, and
`instance-of(constructible-universe, reflective-formal-system)` is denied by
both cards concerned.

The star is the stance: *where a term has no definition, say which statements
are theorems, which are usage and which were coined here*.

## Territory

The cards with a claim under this star, and the claims:

```hql
from claims import under

cards
| flat_map(under)
| filter(u => u.star == "notion-not-class")
| group(by = u => u.card)
| sort(by = g => g.key)
| map(g => {card: g.key, claims: g.items | map(u => u.ref)})
```

## Where it strains

This star is the reason the claims carry evidence grades at all. It is also
the star least expressed in the logic, eleven atoms for twenty-four claims,
since a claim about the status of a definition fits no predicate. What it
abstracts is mostly still prose.
